            <ArticleTitle>[Effect of the spinal pain syndrome on pain reactions caused by nociceptive thermal stimuli].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>149-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>It has been shown that the reaction of both limbs to thermal pain stimulation was suppressed during spinal pain syndrome development caused by generators of pathologically enhanced excitation (GPEE) formed in the dorsal horns of the spinal cord lumbosacral segments on one side. The analgetic effect on physiological pain was retained long after pain syndrome disappearance (48 hours), the effect was bilateral and was independent of the type of agent producing GPEE. It was shown that neuronal activity in the antinociceptive system key structure (nucleus raphe dorsal) increases. It is assumed that physiological pain relief is caused by enhanced activity in antinociceptive system structures in pain syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
